   112	**1** **INTRODUCTION**
   113	
   114	
   115	Modern software utilizes multiple programming languages [ 17 ],
   116	leveraging their respective features and strengths for enhanced
   117	functionality and efficiency. Scripting languages such as JavaScript
   118	are commonly used for frontend, object-oriented languages like C#
   119	or Java for backend, and low-level languages such as C, C++, or
   120	WebAssembly for performance-critical computations. Code written
   121	in multiple languages is composed through _cross-language interac-_
   122	_tions_ like calling foreign-language functions and passing values.
   123	Although multi-language software is widespread, state-of-theart static analysis frameworks analyze a single language only [ 7,
   124	8, 25, 29 ]. Even though some frameworks use summaries for native methods in the Java Class Library, they disregard other crosslanguage interactions, leading to unsound analysis results [ 7, 25, 29 ],
   125	or imprecisely over-approximate possible effects of this interaction [5], rendering the analysis results useless.
   126	Cross-language analyses analyze multi-language programs across
   127	language boundaries. We identified the following four different approaches for developing cross-language analyses that either:
   128	
   129	
   130	(1) use handcrafted summaries for foreign function calls,
   131	(2) implement all analyses into a single framework,
   132	(3) reify summaries, or
   133	(4) translate all languages into a common representation.
   134	
   135	
   136	As we elaborate in Section 5, they all have limitations: (1) handcrafted summaries [ 7, 10, 12 ] are time-consuming and prone to
   137	becoming outdated; (2) cross-language analyses within a single
   138	framework [ 3, 15, 21, 30 ] require significant effort to implement for
   139	unsupported languages; (3) summary extraction and reification [ 2, 4,
   140	19, 27 ] struggles with implicit boundaries and cyclic dependencies;
   141	and (4) translating code to a common representation [ 6, 14, 22,
   142	26, 28 ] loses high-level information, reducing analysis precision.
   143	These problems can be addressed through the integration of existing
   144	single-language analyses implemented in different frameworks.
   145	In this paper, we present such an approach: the _AXA_ architecture, an **A** rchitecture for **Cross** -language **A** nalysis. By reusing
   146	
   147	
   148	ASE ’24, October 27-November 1, 2024, Sacramento, CA, USA Tobias Roth, Julius Näumann, Dominik Helm, Sven Keidel, and Mira Mezini
   149	
   150	
   151	
   152	existing analyses, our approach does not require hand-crafted summaries and avoids duplicating implementation effort. Moreover, our
   153	approach analyzes each language by language-specialized analyses and, as a result, does not need to reify summaries. Lastly, our
   154	approach does not lose precision through translation to an intermediate representation because it analyzes each language in a suitable
   155	representation.
   156	AXA comprises the following distinct components: First, AXA
   157	augments existing single-language analyses through _language de-_
   158	_tectors_ designed to identify cross-language interactions like foreign
   159	function calls and foreign memory accesses. Second, _translators_
   160	convert analysis results between different single-language analyses. Third, a central _coordinator_ orchestrates the interleaved execution of single-language analyses and propagates results between
   161	them. Crucially, the individual analyses are executed in an interleaved manner, exchanging intermediate results as soon as they
   162	become available, thus computing a global fixed-point solution.
   163	Finally, _connectors_ wrap the reused single-language analyses, providing a unified interface for the coordinator to start and resume
   164	them. The coordinator is analysis- and framework-independent and
   165	reusable across different cross-language analyses. Connectors are
   166	framework-specific, but need to be implemented only once for each
   167	integrated analysis framework. Translators and language detectors
   168	are framework- and analysis-specific. Yet, our evaluation shows
   169	that the effort of implementing translators and language detectors
   170	is negligible compared to the complexity of state-of-the-art static
   171	analysis frameworks.
   172	We evaluate AXA by developing a cross-language points-to analysis for Java interacting with embedded JavaScript code and native
   173	code compiled to LLVM bitcode. We implement this cross-language
   174	analysis by integrating an existing Java analysis from the OPAL
   175	framework [ 7 ], a JavaScript analysis from the TAJS framework [ 8 ],
   176	and an LLVM analysis from the SVF framework [ 27 ]. Each of these
   177	analyses represents the respective state of the art and their performance and precision has been fine-tuned over many years.
   178	Our evaluation shows that AXA allows the reuse of more than
   179	
   180	98% of lines of code of the existing analyses. Furthermore, we validate that our analysis can handle all relevant Java-JavaScript and
   181	Java-Native cross-language interaction patterns. For this purpose,
   182	we use a hand-crafted benchmark for the cross-language interactions we identified. Finally, we show that our cross-language
   183	analysis significantly improves the recall over the integrated singlelanguage analyses without compromising precision.
   184	In summary, our contributions are:
   185	
   186	
   187	  - AXA, an **A** rchitecture for **cross** -language **A** nalysis that integrates existing single-language analyses modularly. AXA
   188	allows building upon existing, carefully fine-tuned, state-ofthe-art analyses implemented in different frameworks. AXA
   189	works for multi-language programs with implicit language
   190	boundaries and dynamically generated code and does not
   191	sacrifice precision by translating multi-language programs
   192	to a common representation.
   193	
   194	  - A cross-language pointer and call-graph analysis based on
   195	AXA for Java with embedded JavaScript and native LLVM
   196	code, integrating existing single-language analyses from
   197	OPAL, TAJS, and SVF.
