   392	
   393	
   394	components (cf. Section 3.1) before elaborating on how they interact with each other (cf. Section 3.2). In Section 3.3, we describe
   395	requirements to analysis frameworks for integration into AXA.
   396	
   397	
   398	**3.1** **Components**
   399	
   400	
   401	Existing single-language analyses are agnostic of cross-language
   402	interactions ( **Challenge 1** ). To address this issue, AXA extends
   403	each single-language analysis to be integrated with one _detector_ for
   404	each language it interacts with. For example, _analysis_ _J_ is extended
   405	with _detector_ _J,JS_ for JavaScript and _detector_ _J,N_ for native code.
   406	To cope with different internal representations of the results of
   407	the integrated single-language analyses ( **Challenge 2** ), AXA introduces _translators_ . For instance, _translator_ _JS,J_ translates between
   408	representations of _analysis_ _JS_ and _analysis_ _J_ .
   409	To resolve mutual dependencies across language boundaries
   410	( **Challenge 3** ), AXA introduces a central _coordinator_ that executes
   411	the integrated analyses in an interleaved manner rather than in
   412	a fixed sequence. To allow the coordinator to interact with the
   413	integrated analyses, AXA wraps each integrated analysis into a
   414	connector (e.g., _connector_ _J_ for _analysis_ _J_ ) that implements a uniform
   415	interface.
   416	
   417	Overall, AXA is similar to a blackboard architecture [ 23 ], with
   418	the integrated single-language analyses being the experts and the
   419	coordinator and its central store being the blackboard over which
   420	the experts indirectly share results to collaboratively solve a multilanguage analysis problem.
   421	We now describe each of these components in detail.
